                <h1>About Me</h1>
                <p>I'm an AI researcher and software engineer specializing in <strong>computer vision</strong>, <strong>deep learning</strong>, and <strong>graphics programming</strong>. My work bridges academic research with practical engineering, from developing neural networks for remote physiological monitoring to building custom game engines in DirectX and OpenGL.</p>
                <p>Currently exploring <strong>procedural 3D generation</strong> and co-founding <strong>Pecus Chain</strong>. I was previously VP of Product &amp; Co-founder at <strong>ClusTEQ</strong>, a BIM platform built on game-engine technology, until July 2026. My research has been published in academic venues, focusing on contactless health monitoring, NLP bias detection, and ethical AI systems.</p>

                <h2>Off Screen</h2>
                <p>When I'm not working, I try to get as far from a screen as I reasonably can. Most of that time goes to the <strong>gym</strong>, a small <strong>garden</strong> I'm slowly getting better at not killing, and an ongoing obsession with <strong>fermentation</strong> &mdash; sourdough, kombucha, and the occasional jar of kimchi that ends up taking over the kitchen counter. There's something about a process you can't rush or debug your way through that's a good counterweight to a day spent writing code.</p>
                <p>None of it happens in isolation, either. I've got a small group of <strong>close friends</strong> I've kept for years, and a good chunk of the gardening, fermenting, and gym time doubles as an excuse to see them.</p>
